{
 "id": "l04",
 "label": "4",
 "title": "The markers si and ang",
 "summary": "How to mark who or what a sentence is about, and how to show more than one",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Use si, sina, ang and ang mga before the subject of a sentence",
  "Build simple sentences with ay",
  "Show plurals the Tagalog way, including plural describing words",
  "Use ang before names of places"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Every subject needs a marker",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In Tagalog, the person or thing a sentence is about almost always has a small **marker** in front of it. English has no exact match for this. You can say \"Ana is happy\", but in Tagalog you must say something like \"**si** Ana is happy\"."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Marker",
      "Used before",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[si|marker]]",
       "the name of one person",
       "**Si** Ana, **Si** Ben"
      ],
      [
       "[[sina|marker]]",
       "the names of two or more people",
       "**Sina** Ana at Ben"
      ],
      [
       "[[ang|marker]]",
       "one thing, place or animal",
       "**Ang** bata, **Ang** aso"
      ],
      [
       "[[ang mga|marker]]",
       "two or more things, places or animals",
       "**Ang mga** bata, **Ang mga** aso"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Four markers for the subject"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "A basic sentence with ay",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Pattern",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Si",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "Ana",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ay",
         "r": "ay"
        },
        {
         "t": "babae",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "Subject first, then ay, then what is said about it. Ay means is or are."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Si Ana ay babae.",
       "en": "Ana is a woman.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ana",
         "Ana",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "babae",
         "woman",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Si Ben ay marunong.",
       "en": "Ben is clever.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ben",
         "Ben",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "marunong",
         "clever",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ang bata ay mabait.",
       "en": "The child is kind.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bata",
         "child",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "mabait",
         "kind",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ang kape ay mainit.",
       "en": "The coffee is hot.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "kape",
         "coffee",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "mainit",
         "hot",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Tap each sentence to hear it"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "The rule",
     "t": "A noun that is the subject of a sentence needs its marker. The only exceptions are when you call a person by name (\"Ana, come here!\") and when you give a command."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "English comparison",
     "t": "English also has markers: **the** and **a**. The difference is that Tagalog markers are required for names too, and there are different ones for one person, several people, one thing and several things."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "More than one: sina and ang mga",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Use **sina** before two or more names. Use **ang mga** before more than one thing. The word **mga** is said **ma-NGA**, and on its own it means nothing. Its only job is to show \"more than one\"."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Sina Ana at Ben ay mababait.",
       "en": "Ana and Ben are kind.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Sina",
         "marks 2+ names",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ana",
         "Ana",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "Ben",
         "Ben",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "mababait",
         "kind (plural form)",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ang mga bata ay masaya.",
       "en": "The children are happy.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "mga",
         "more than one",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bata",
         "child",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "masaya",
         "happy",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ang mga bulaklak ay maganda.",
       "en": "The flowers are beautiful.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "mga",
         "more than one",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bulaklak",
         "flower",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "are",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "maganda",
         "beautiful",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "The plural is optional on both sides"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog has a flexible plural rule. Either the subject or the describing word can show \"more than one\", and showing it on both is allowed but optional."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Sentence",
      "Meaning",
      "Plural shown on"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Ang bata ay mabait.",
       "The child is kind.",
       "nowhere (one child)"
      ],
      [
       "Ang **mga** bata ay mabait.",
       "The children are kind.",
       "the subject only"
      ],
      [
       "Ang **mga** bata ay **mababait**.",
       "The children are kind.",
       "subject and describing word"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Choosing where to show the plural"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Which one to use?",
     "t": "All the plural sentences are correct. Showing the plural on both words sounds the most complete, and it is a good habit while you are learning."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Describing words in the plural",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Most describing words that start with **ma-** form the plural by **repeating the first syllable of the root**. The root is the part after ma-."
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "The plural recipe",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Singular",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "ma",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "bait",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Plural",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "ma",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "ba",
         "r": "pron"
        },
        {
         "t": "bait",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "mabait → mababait. The extra ba is the repeated first syllable of the root bait."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Singular",
      "Plural",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[mabait|pred]]",
       "[[mababait|pred]]  (ma-ba-ba-IT)",
       "kind"
      ],
      [
       "[[maliit|pred]]",
       "[[maliliit|pred]]  (ma-li-li-IT)",
       "small"
      ],
      [
       "[[malaki|pred]]",
       "[[malalaki|pred]]  (ma-la-la-KI)",
       "big"
      ],
      [
       "[[maganda|pred]]",
       "[[magaganda|pred]]  (ma-ga-gan-DA)",
       "beautiful"
      ],
      [
       "[[masaya|pred]]",
       "[[masasaya|pred]]  (ma-sa-SA-ya)",
       "happy"
      ],
      [
       "[[malinis|pred]]",
       "[[malilinis|pred]]  (ma-li-li-NIS)",
       "clean"
      ],
      [
       "[[masipag|pred]]",
       "[[masisipag|pred]]  (ma-si-SI-pag)",
       "hardworking"
      ],
      [
       "[[marunong|pred]]",
       "[[marurunong|pred]]  (ma-ru-RU-nong)",
       "clever"
      ],
      [
       "[[mahirap|pred]]",
       "[[mahihirap|pred]]  (ma-hi-HI-rap)",
       "poor, difficult"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Plural describing words"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "warn",
     "title": "Not every describing word follows the recipe",
     "t": "Words that do not begin with ma-, such as **bago** (new) or **mura** (cheap), keep the same form. You will learn them as you go."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Names of places also take ang",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Places are treated like things, so they take **ang**. This includes countries, cities and towns."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Ang Australya ay malaki.",
       "en": "Australia is big.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Australya",
         "Australia",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "malaki",
         "big",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ang Maynila ay punong-lungsod ng Pilipinas.",
       "en": "Manila is the capital city of the Philippines.",
       "parts": [
        [
